  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Human rights defence and education for democracy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Chiapas is one of the poorest regions of Mexico with poverty indexes comparable with many low income countries in other continents of the world. The historic economic marginalisation and political repression of its people has created chronic social tensions that exploded in January 1994 and led to nearly a decade of armed conflict and military occupation.  This time has been peppered with massacres and the mass displacement of the civilian population.  Although there have been efforts at dialogue and peace-building, led by the Catholic Church and encouraged by the international community, there has been little evident progress or improvement in the human rights situation. The Mexican government has shown a consistent lack of political will to address the fundamental needs and concerns of the indigenous population, regarding land security, autonomy and the respect for traditional forms of self-organisation.  At the same time, the lack of legal redress against the operation of paramilitary groups linked to powerful economic interests continues to threaten and destabilise the lives of the local people. The Fray Bartolom&#xE9; Human Rights Centre is a major player in Mexico, with a strong public and advocacy profile, and which serves as one of the main reference points for human rights defence and peace-building in the region.
Frayba&#xB4;s work will support indigenous communities and social organisations to defend and exercise the rights of Indigenous Peoples and the Right to Justice for the excluded and organized people of the region. It will do this through accompaniment and strengthening of organizational and community level processes, the promotion of greater linkages and articulation with other organizations at national and international level and the issuing of urgent actions and public statements to ensure the State fulfills its responsibility to respect, protect and promote human rights.</narrative>
  </description>
